1,0 $ With a partial veil that forms a ring on the stem; cap yellow. $ Pluteus mammillatus
1,2 $ Partial veil and ring absent; cap variously colored.
2,3 $ Stem bruising and discoloring bluish to greenish.
2,4 $ Stem not bluing.
3,0 $ Cap wrinkled in the center; cap margin lined; gills well spaced, their edges not discoloring; odor and taste mild; spores 6-7 x 5-6 µ; cystidia without spines. $ Pluteus cyanopus
3,0 $ Cap center not wrinkled; cap margin not lined; gills crowded, their edges discoloring grayish; odor and taste unpleasant; spores 7-9 x 5-6 µ; cystidia with spines. $ Pluteus salicinus
4,5 $ Cap bright red to orange.
4,7 $ Cap otherwise colored.
5,0 $ Cap tiny (1-1.5 cm across) at maturity, scarlet when young but eventually "almost striped yellow on more orange ground" (Singer); recorded from Florida, Cuba, and Trinidad. $ Pluteus laetifrons
5,6 $ Not completely as above (not : Cap tiny (1-1.5 cm across) at maturity, scarlet when young but eventually "almost striped yellow on more orange ground" )
6,0 $ Cap bright red, fading to orange, 2-5 cm across, margin not lined or only slightly lined; cystidia 44-76 µ long; known from the Great Lake States, northeastern North America, and Colorado--possibly widely distributed. $ Pluteus aurantiorugosus
6,0 $ Cap "deep orange yellow," 2 cm across (according to the only official measurement), margin lined; cystidia 28-50 µ long; known from New York, apparently only documented once, in 1903. $ Pluteus aurantiacus
7,8 $ Gill edges dark (brown to gray or blackish) at maturity, contrasting with the faces.
7,13 $ Gill edges not as above.(not : Gill edges dark (brown to gray or blackish) at maturity, contrasting with the faces )
8,0 $ Cap small (up to 2 cm wide) at maturity, grayish; reported from South America and perhaps to be expected in tropical and subtropical North America. $ Pluteus luctuosus
8,9 $ Cap larger than above at maturity, variously colored; variously distributed.
9,0 $ Odor of crushed flesh strong and pungent, "reminding one of crushed inner bark of elderberry" (Smith); cap dark brown, with appressed fibers; spores 7-8 x 5-5.5 µ; cystidia with poorly developed spines at their apices; known from the Great Lakes region. $ Pluteus bartelliae
9,10 $ Not completely as above (not : Odor of crushed flesh strong and pungent, "reminding one of crushed inner bark of elderberry" (Smith); cap dark brown, with appressed fibers; spores 7-8 x 5-5.5 µ; cystidia with poorly developed spines at their apices).
10,0 $ Cap smooth, sticky, pinkish gray with a darker center; tropical in distribution. $ Pluteus spinulosus
10,11 $ Not completely as above.(not : Cap smooth, sticky, pinkish gray with a darker center; tropical in distribution.)
11,0 $ Growing on the wood of hardwoods; cap velvety, color varying from white to brown to reddish brown. $ Pluteus eugraptus
11,12 $ Growing on the wood of conifers; cap not velvety (though possibly with appressed fibers), color grayish or dark brown.
12,0 $ Cap with a conspicuously wrinkled center, dark gray to grayish brown or dark brown; spores 5-7 x 3-4 µ; cystidia with thin walls and tiny irregular projections. $ Pluteus umbrosusphoto only
12,0 $ Cap without a conspicuously wrinkled center, dark brown; spores 6.5-8 x 4.5-5 µ; cystidia with thick walls and large apical projections. $ Pluteus atromarginatus
13,0 $ Growing on the wood of hardwoods; stem long and slender (4-10 cm x 4-8 mm); cap brownish overall when young but soon yellow on the margin and progressively yellowing toward the center, the surface finely granular; gills white before turning pink; cystidia without spines. $ Pluteus flavofuligineus
13,14 $ Not completely as above.(not : Growing on the wood of hardwoods; stem long and slender (4-10 cm x 4-8 mm); cap brownish overall when young but soon yellow on the margin and progressively yellowing toward the center, the surface finely granular; gills white before turning pink; cystidia without spines.)
14,15 $ Cap with yellow to greenish or olive shades.
14,22 $ Cap without yellow, greenish, or olive shades.
15,0 $ Cap tiny, "yellow with smoky reticulated veins radiating from the center" (Saccardo); gills white before maturity; stem with a fuzzy base, probably white overall (bad punctuation in the original description makes this unclear); microscopic characters unknown; found on hickory logs in the 19th Century, somewhere in North America. (Note: This species is obviously poorly documented. It has been synonymized with Pluteus admirabilis by some authors, and with Pluteus chrysophaeus by others. Some authors maintain the species. The brief description of the cap surface does seem distinctive.) $ Pluteus chrysophlebius
15,16 $ Not completely as above.(not : Cap tiny, "yellow with smoky reticulated veins radiating from the center" (Saccardo); gills white before maturity; stem with a fuzzy base, probably white overall (bad punctuation in the original description makes this unclear); microscopic characters unknown; found on hickory logs in the 19th Century, somewhere in North America. (Note: This species is obviously poorly documented. It has been synonymized with Pluteus admirabilis by some authors, and with Pluteus chrysophaeus by others. Some authors maintain the species. The brief description of the cap surface does seem distinctive.))
16,0 $ Cap white overall, with a yellowish center; gills white before maturity; stem white; probably tropical in distribution (originally collected in Jamaica). $ Pluteus myceniformis
16,17 $ Not completely as above.(not : Cap white overall, with a yellowish center; gills white before maturity; stem white;)
17,18 $ Cap uniformly yellow.
17,20 $ Cap greenish, greenish gray, olive, or brownish with yellow shades mixed in.
18,0 $ Cap large (10 cm across); stem white; probably tropical in distribution (originally collected in Cuba). $ Pluteus earliae
18,19 $ Cap smaller than above (1-3 cm across); stem yellow; recorded from temperate North America.
19,0 $ Cap and stem bright lemon yellow; gills white at first but soon yellowish before turning pinkish. (Note: The European species Pluteus leoninus has a yellow cap and a stem variously described as yellow, white, or both; some North American sources use the name to make distinctions in stem color and/or cap surface texture.) $ Pluteus admirabilis
19,0 $ Cap and stem honey yellow; gills white at first and not becoming yellowish before maturity. $ Pluteus melleus
