61,0 $ Recorded from Washington in 1911 on the wood of alder--and perhaps found once in Michigan by Singer, though Smith and Stuntz think not, due to the lack of thick-walled cystidia in the cells of the fibrils at the base of the stem in Singer's Michigan collection . . . you get the picture. $ Pluteus latifolius
61,0 $ Recorded consistently from eastern North America on the wood of hardwoods or conifers. $ Pluteus granularis
62,63 $ Cap center conspicuously radially veined.
62,64 $ Cap center not conspicuously veined.
